+
+## Parameter sensitivity
+
+Figures 9-11 exist to answer the obvious question about a model with this
+many free parameters: how much of the result is the mechanism and how much is
+the particular numbers in Appendix 5.1?
+
+Every free parameter is scaled (gains and precisions) or biased (the
+log-precisions) away from its published value while `M1` and `M2` stay exactly
+as published, and the switch is detected again — `switch_metrics` requires
+`M1` to be the experienced hypothesis for the whole visible period and `M2` to
+take over for at least a second once the hand is covered, so a model that was
+illusory from the start does not count as having switched.
+
+In Fig. 9 the line is the mean switch time over 10 noise realisations and the
+ribbon is ±1 s.d.; the strip along the bottom of each panel marks grid points
+where every realisation switched (filled square), only some did (hollow) or
+none did (grey cross). Fig. 11 adds a second row showing `U(M1) - U(M2)`,
+because the two uncertainty curves are only ~0.05 apart before covering and
+~0.5 apart after it, so on a shared axis the crossing itself is invisible.
+
+The switch is preserved over a wide range of every parameter, and it also has
+limits, which are as informative as the robustness:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| `k_lam` above ~2.8× | `M2` gets ahead *before* the hand is covered — a fast enough precision learner is illusory from the start |
+| `P_lam` on `Av` above ~1e3× | the agent cannot revise its estimate of visual reliability, so no switch — the continuous version of Fig. 4 |
+| `lam_covered` on `Av` above ~+7 | the occlusion is too mild to register as a precision change |
+| `k_state` below ~0.01×, `P_X` above ~100× | state estimation is too slow, resp. the state prior swamps the sensory evidence |
+
+Note that `k_state` and `P_X` do nothing at all near their published values,
+and this is not a numerical accident: `expm(K_X*H*p.dt)` in `simulate` has
+already underflowed to zero there, so the state update *is* the exact Newton
+step and its gain cannot matter, and `P_X = 1` is added to an `M'*Pz*M` whose
+entries are of order `1e3`. Both need sweeping over orders of magnitude before
+they bite, which is what `sensitivity_spec` does.
 
 ## Code layout
 
